Emergency Service Dispatch System (ESDS)

Project Description
The Emergency Service Dispatch System (ESDS) is a Java desktop application designed to register, organize, and manage emergency incidents.

The system allows users to register emergency reports, assign priority levels, search incidents using their Incident ID, and dispatch the highest-priority pending incident.

This project demonstrates the application of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) and Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA).

 Features

- Incident Registration – Register emergency incidents with reporter information, contact number, incident type, location, description, and priority.
- Input Validation – Checks required fields and validates the information before registration.
- Automatic Incident ID – Generates a unique ID for every registered incident.
- Priority Assignment – Allows users to update the priority of a pending incident.
- Highest-Priority Dispatching – Automatically selects and dispatches the pending incident with the highest priority.
- Search by Incident ID – Finds incident records using Manual Linear Search.
- Incident Records – Displays registered incidents and their current status.

Incident Types

The system supports three types of emergency incidents:

- Medical
- Fire
- Accident

Priority Levels

The system follows this priority order:

1. Critical
2. High
3. Medium
4. Low

Object-Oriented Programming (IT21)

The project applies the following OOP concepts:

- Encapsulation – Uses private fields to protect incident information.
- Constructors – Initializes incident objects.
- Getters and Setters – Provides controlled access to incident information.
- Inheritance – Uses a parent class and child classes.

Class Structure

Parent Class:
- `Incident`

Child Classes:
- `MedicalIncident`
- `FireIncident`
- `AccidentIncident`

The child classes extend the `Incident` parent class and use `super()` to call its constructor.

Data Structures and Algorithms (IT20)

The project implements the following:

- Array-based Priority Queue – Stores and manages incidents according to their priority levels.
- Manual Linear Search – Searches for an incident by checking stored records one by one using a loop.
- Array Storage – Uses an array with a maximum capacity of 100 incidents.
- Priority Comparison – Compares priority levels to identify the highest-priority pending incident.

The main data structure and searching logic are manually implemented without using Java Collection classes.

Technologies Used

- Java
- Java Swing (GUI)
- Apache Maven
- NetBeans IDE

How to Run

1. Clone or download this repository.
2. Open NetBeans IDE.
3. Select File → Open Project.
4. Locate and open the `EmergencyServiceDispatch` project folder.
5. Build and run the project.

